docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:33:| P20 | Key rotation issues a new key with a 24-hour grace period, a later revocation and a secret recoverable from the Keychain (`REVIEWS.key`, `keyRotated/keySecretClosed/keyDeployed/keyDoneAt`) | Two admitted operations in sequence: `ApiKeys · Version` (additional key, one-time reveal through the existing `RevealSecret` host sheet, never persisted) → local deployment acknowledgement → `ApiKeys · Delete` of the old key with readback. Two distinct timestamps; a lost secret means "issue another key" | `changes.rs` 842–846 and `management.rs` 980–988 define Rotate (regenerate, no grace) and Version (additional key); DESIGN.md "Secrets" forbids persisting issued keys |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:35:| P22 | Red Team Targets tab lists target names (`rtm` tables) | Targets shows `target_id` and `target_type` only | The C13 contract (`red_team.rs` 5–8, 56–66) deliberately keeps target names out of the core's reads; the privacy boundary is not widened for presentation |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:38:| P25 | Revision of P1/P2: "secrets enter only through a host secret sheet" | The retained, reviewed entry path (C02/C09) is a webview password field read through a ref, cleared immediately and sent once inside the intent as `EnteredSecret` (zeroized, never in React state, never logged); the host owns the *reveal* sheet (`RevealSecret`) and the clipboard. C29 uses exactly this path for the scan key, management secret and Gateway key; no new host entry sheet is built | The C27 spec assumed an entry sheet the host does not have; the reviewed pattern already satisfies D014 and the C24 canary checks (no secret in any AX tree or file) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:39:| P26 | `verifyTenantSetup` as one core intent streaming `VerifyStep`s (wizard.md) | Implemented as specified: `verifyTenantSetup{context}` runs in the core over the draft, admits the reads it needs through the management pipeline (management verify; the chosen Gateway workspace's prerequisites, or only the listing when none is chosen, reported as incomplete coverage; Red Team; model/skill) and settles each line from the result; AIS/AES are listed as not available and never read; the lines, `done`, `failed`, `blocking` and `stale` stream through `ShellSnapshot.setup.verify`. The scan key has no admitted read: its line is `stored`, never `pass`. Only the scan key (first run) blocks; an optional service's failure is carried as a warning the operator may continue over | Attempt-01 composed the reads in the presentation; attempt-02 read only the workspace listing and blocked on every failure — both rejected |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:40:| P27 | Add-tenant Cancel writes nothing except an already-vaulted credential | The wizard works on a core-owned draft (`beginTenantSetup`) with its own context and its own Gateway listing and choice; credentials attach to the draft; nothing is listed as a tenant (a draft id is refused by `selectTenant`/`deleteTenant`), the active tenant and its Gateway choice are untouched until `completeTenantSetup` commits the draft in one step and switches once. Every change to the draft's credentials or profile retires its context (and the binding, reads and listing under it). Cancel (`discardTenantSetup`) drops the draft, retires the binding and every read under its context and workspace reference, and queues its Keychain records for cleanup; the sheet closes only once the core admits it (a creation still pending or unconfirmed is awaited and whatever it produced is discarded). A relaunch, or a tenant committed outside the wizard during first run, does the same | Stronger than the spec's leftover credential: no orphan secrets and no cached authority surviving a replaced credential |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:41:| P28 | Gateway workspace key entered on the wizard tenant step | Implemented: an optional section reads the workspaces through the management client (`management` gateway inventory on the draft context), `selectGatewayWorkspace` chooses one, and `configureInferenceCredential` saves the endpoint and key for it | The admitted sequence (list → select → key) runs on the draft like on a tenant |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:43:| P30 | The wizard opens whenever onboarding is not done | The shell shows onboarding as done whenever a tenant already exists (an upgrade, or a tenant created before the v2 shell was chosen); the stored state is untouched. A first run is one with no tenant yet; a relaunch mid-setup reopens at the Tenant step with a fresh draft (the previous draft's credentials are cleaned up) | Existing users must never be sent through the wizard; a half-made draft is never a tenant |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:44:| P31 | Products step shows licence state read from the tenant | No admitted service reports licensing (DESIGN-V2 §6); each card shows what it reads with (scan key / management client, saved or not) and, once verified, that product's verify result; a note says licence state is not readable through the public APIs | Nothing may be inferred about licensing from reads that do not report it |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:46:| P33 | `ReadStatus` as its own core scope with per-product states from dedicated reads | `readTenant` admits one finite read per chosen product through the existing management pipeline: the Runtime overview the shell already owns (sessions chart, key inventory, the chosen Gateway workspace's usage and limits), the Red Team statistics and scan list (C13's read), the model scans, skill statistics and skill scans (C13's read); each settles into the tenant's read record as a per-source outcome (read, needs a role, failed); a source counts as read only when it established what it is for: a listing whose page is the inventory (not capped, nothing beyond the first page), a sessions chart with consistent totals, a key page with every expiration reported, Gateway usage with the day's requests and errors both known; anything less is named in `sourcesMissing` (the same gaps the Inbox shows). A product attempt that established nothing (every source failed, or the read never reached the service) is kept as the latest failed attempt beside the product's last outcome that did, which stays shown as older; the read is failed while such an attempt is the latest (a missing role is a settled attempt reported on the product, not a failed read). Once anything was attempted, every chosen source nothing has reached yet is named as unread coverage too, and `settledAt` carries the time of the latest attempt so a completion that established nothing is announced for when it settled, never as a first read still pending. A healthy empty read older than the ten-minute window (`OverviewView.fresh`, C12) is a gap: Home and the Inbox keep the cached read and name its age instead of an all-clear, never re-reading on their own; a refresh in progress keeps that read and its age named until the new read lands (admission restores nothing). A chosen Gateway with no workspace is unread coverage, named in `sourcesMissing`; `recheckProduct` re-reads one chosen product. The read completes (one generation) when every part admitted under the tenant's current context has landed; a read in which no source answered is a failed read (`failed`, with `failedAt`), never pending and never done, and a failed refresh keeps the earlier read labelled as older | No second read pipeline, and Home never claims coverage a read did not establish (I4): revised after the C30 attempt-01 review, which found selected Red Team and model/skill products left unread and a workspace-less Gateway announced as complete |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:47:| P34 | Inbox items with `kind`, `severity` and `explanation` per item | `InboxEntry` carries the overview item (Runtime, Gateway) or the C29 setup warning with its hold (listed, with every action, whatever the read status: a warning exists before any read); the explanation is per kind in the presentation's copy, not invented per item; `deadline` is absent (P20). Its action carries an `InboxTarget` (destination, product, Administration inventory, item name): a key item opens Administration on the API keys inventory filtered to the key, a setup warning opens where it is fixed (Settings for the scan key or management client, AI Gateway, AI Red Teaming) | The admitted reads report no per-item explanation or deadline; the target was added after the attempt-01 review found actions landing on the destination's default view |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:48:| P35 | Guard offers "Archive and switch" / "Discard and switch" | Archive ends the conversation (its protected history stays as C20 saved it); Discard ends it, removes its protected history record (after any save under way, under the same lock the sink holds) and refuses every later save of it (the turn the stop cuts short), then drops it once its task returns; both then switch once; the pending approval is never decided for the operator; the dialog's initial focus is Stay, the safe default (Enter on arrival ends nothing). A save admitted before the discard (the turn the engine's end cut short) re-checks the tombstone under the history hold before it writes, so it can never write the discarded transcript back after the removal. A removal that fails (storage unavailable, access denied) is never implied to have succeeded: the record stays listed and the workspace's history reports the failure, through unrelated opens and removals, until the operator removes the record | The engine is stopped either way: a switch retires its binding (C20); the difference is whether the transcript is kept, which the dialog promises and the attempt-01 review found unkept |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:50:| P37 | Scopes are per tenant and a switch round trip shows the cached state | Each opened tenant owns its read record in the core (its last overview, the other products' outcomes, generation, recheck, snooze receipt) for the session; a switch away and back shows it again without a new read. Per-operation bookkeeping is separate from the cached data: each read part is tracked with the context it was admitted under, "reading" means a part under the current context is open, a part under a retired context is withdrawn when it lands (never recorded, never counted), so a read held open across a switch round trip can never leave the tenant reading. The presentation's own transient state follows the tenant too: the shell store carries the shown scope (set from each snapshot before it renders); a toast for a tenant's item or read and an unconfirmed delivery bound to a context are refused at insertion when that scope is no longer shown (a refusal or lost delivery arriving after a switch), retired on leaving it (however long Reduce Motion would keep them) and filtered at render; a contextual arrival belongs to the tenant it was followed in | I2 names the tenant, not the context, as the owner of its cached scopes; the attempt-01 review found the overview bound to the context and lost across A → B → A |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:51:| P38 | Snooze toast states the core-computed return time | The core issues a `SnoozeReceipt{id, title, returnsAt, sequence}` on the tenant's Inbox scope for the last snooze placed in the session; the shell announces a new sequence once, in the tenant it was placed in, and never a hold a switch or a reread merely rediscovers; a snooze ends at its return time whether or not its entry is actionable, and the core's deadline ticker publishes the expiry (the holds in effect are part of the overview's time signature) | I11 wants one source for the return time and one announcement per snooze; diffing holds between snapshots announced old holds after a switch (attempt-01 review) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:52:| P39 | `AdminInventory` generated from screens/administration-inventory.md | Implemented as a Rust table (`admin_inventory::inventory`) carried on `ShellSnapshot.administration` in the v2 shell only (null in the legacy shell, whose literal snapshot and leak canary are unchanged; the presentation's fixture copy is pinned to the table by a core test); a unit test and the `ui_v2_reviews` suite pin every offered (kind, action) to `changes::is_allowed` and every denied pair to its refusal, so a label can never outrun the core. Read and Change tabs render the retained views (the same reads and reviewed changes) embedded under the accordion with the inventory's labels; Unavailable tabs and products show their gate and nothing else; Removed tabs are not rendered. The Gateway MCP servers tab lists the chosen workspace's catalog through `listMcpServers` | One source of truth for dispositions, checked against the matrices the core enforces (V2-T20, V2-R11) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:53:| P40 | `openReview`/`confirmReview{typed}` intents | The retained review intents are the review (`reviewChange` binds tenant, context, credentials and the read baseline; `requestApply{confirmation}` validates the typed name in the core and opens the native confirmation, whose private completion alone applies). The v2 sheet disables Apply until the typed text equals the review's name and renders the applied panel only from the core's result and readback | The C09 review already carries every binding I3 asks for; new intents would duplicate it |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:54:| P41 | Rotation state per tenant with two stored timestamps | `Tenant.rotations` (persisted, never a secret): driven from settled `ApiKeys` results of the active context (a Version read back starts one; an unknown Version starts an unconfirmed one; a failed one records nothing; the old key's Delete read back absent completes it; a rejected Delete keeps it with the reason; an unknown one is reconciled by a complete key inventory read), plus `acknowledgeDeployment{application}` stored once. The reminder is the Inbox item `rotation-<application>` while a key is confirmed and the old one not read back gone. The one-time secret stays on its change result for the context it was applied in: Reveal is offered only while the core holds it; any sheet exit forgets it and a context change drops it | The issued secret must never be persisted (I6) while the stage and timestamps must survive reads, switches and relaunches (I7) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:57:| P44 | Lifecycle conversations are seeded data in the prototype (`CONVOS`) | The three lifecycles are fixtures in the contracts package (`fixtures/wire/harness-lifecycle-{blocks,mcp,keys}.json`), one `ConversationView` per step (asking, approved, declined, ended) plus the saved transcript. Each read is one of the desktop's own read tools (`desktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`): blocks `search`, mcp `mcpServers`, keys `inventory` of API keys. They are regression inputs and boards only: a real tenant never shows them (I12) | The core decides approvals; fixtures must be shapes the core emits, validated by `parseShellSnapshot` |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:59:| P46 | `endConversation` returns an archive id | `endHarness` keeps its receipt; the ended conversation names its saved transcript (`ConversationView.historyId`, the protected history record, shown only with save evidence: `savedAt` set and no `saveFailure`) and the rail marks it ended; "Open the saved transcript" opens that record | The archive already exists as the C20 protected history; a second id would duplicate it |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:60:| P47 | The mcp lifecycle reads `GatewayRead::McpServers` (P16) | A new desktop read tool `mcpServers` reads the conversation workspace's MCP catalog (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, the read the MCP panel uses) for the engine, decided in the page like every desktop read | The prototype's AES inventory has no admitted service; the Gateway catalog is the admitted read P16 named |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:61:| P48 | Read-only tool calls are approved in the page (C21 assumed) | Every desktop read tool (inventory, detail, session, search, Red Teaming, model observation, scan, test cases, MCP catalog) now waits for the operator's decision in the page (`ApprovalKind::DesktopRead`, `effect: readOnly`); declined, nothing is read and the engine is told so. Staging a proposal reads nothing and still runs at once. Before C32 these reads ran without asking | The lifecycles' approve and decline paths, and the operator's control over what tenant data reaches the model |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:62:| P49 | The core decides from the tool's write flag whether the native confirmation is required (C21 assumed) | No such branch existed: every decision was in-page. The core now classifies each decision (`HarnessApproval.effect`): a desktop read, or a command while the project is read-only, is `readOnly`; a file change, a command with changes allowed and any workspace MCP tool (whose effect the desktop cannot know; tools carry no trusted annotations) is `mayChange`, and allowing it opens the native confirmation (`Effect::ConfirmHarness`), whose private completion alone sends the allow; refused, the decision stays pending; a stale one is dismissed. In TypeScript `effect` is optional only so pre-C32 snapshots type-check, and absent is treated as `mayChange` | In-page consent must never authorise a change (D058, C32) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:63:| P50 | A composer draft per conversation | One composer per tenant and workspace (by the stable workspace ID): its draft survives navigation, tenant round trips and the retirement of the conversation it was typed for. A message the core did not take is kept under "Not sent"; one whose delivery was not confirmed under "Not confirmed". Each send carries the presentation's own ID (`sendHarness.submission`, 32 hex): the core takes each ID at most once (a repeated delivery after a lost receipt sends nothing again) and reports the sends it took with the message each became and where each stands (`ConversationView.submissions`, newest 64): `pending` (taken, a sign-in renewal or the write confirmation under way: it may still be given back), `delivered` (its message entered the transcript, recorded by the engine session apart from the transcript's bounds) or `returned` (given back, or failed without an engine). Only `delivered` and `returned` settle anything; a tracked send whose conversation is no longer shown before its final word (retired, cleared, another tenant or workspace meanwhile) is kept as Not confirmed with its text. A "Not confirmed" record is settled only by its own conversation reporting its own ID: delivered, it leaves the list (and the box, if it holds exactly that text); given back, it leaves the list and its exact text stays in the box or is kept under Not sent, never cleared; an admitted send is likewise tracked until its conversation reports it, and its receipt never clears text the core already gave back, whichever arrives first; never by matching text, another send's entry, another conversation, or the transcript (which drops its oldest entries past 200 entries or 1 MiB). The core numbers a conversation's messages and never reuses an ID, also when restored. Nothing is sent by itself | A conversation's draft must stay recoverable after the core retires the conversation (C32 reviews) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:64:| P51 | A new investigation prefilled with the ended conversation's opening question | The rail titles the current conversation from its kept opening too, so its title never changes as the transcript drops its oldest entries. A contextual arrival stays pending, bound to its tenant and workspace, until the conversation or the history resolves it ("Finding that investigation…" while the saved conversations are listed); listed without it, or not listable, the page says so; navigation, a new arrival, another workspace or a restart cancels it. The opening question is the conversation's opening message, whole, kept by the core apart from its title (a one-line, shortened label for lists): `ConversationView.opening` and `OpenedConversationView.opening`, captured when the first message (`u-1`) enters the transcript, saved with the record, restored with it and kept after the transcript drops its oldest entries. An archived conversation's record is read for it when the restart is asked for (nothing is filled in meanwhile); choosing another investigation, sending, opening a transcript, a new arrival or another workspace retires that read (a late result changes nothing); otherwise it settles by the core's receipt for the record (`HarnessHistoryView.lastOpen`: id, admission order, failure), newer than any open settled before it was asked for, never by a busy edge or a changed error. When it is not known (a record saved before C32 whose transcript no longer holds its first message) or the record cannot be read, the page says so and never fills in the title instead; a draft in the box is kept under Not sent and nothing is sent | The title is shortened (72 or 80 characters, one line) and lost the rest of a long or multi-line question; the attempt-12 review found the restart prefilling it |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:67:| P54 | Show the calls reveals each action's request and response "as the diagnostics module already redacts them" | The diagnostics module records typed events only. The core now records, in memory for the app run, each call it makes on a tenant's behalf through its transport (scans, management reads and changes with their token requests, AI Gateway inference): purpose, the credential by kind (the core attaches it), method, origin and path without the query (a query is marked withheld), header names (never values), request size, status or failure, duration and response content type, newest 50 per tenant (`ShellSnapshot.calls`, only while the preference is on; forgotten with the tenant), bounded as the contract accepts them (header names of at most 64 characters, at most 128, path cut at 2048 characters, method of at most 16, status 100-999, HTTPS origins only; `CALL_*_LIMIT`). Bodies are never kept: a response can carry an issued key or a token and requests carry tenant content. The API Client's own requests keep their existing redacted history. The preference is the operator's once chosen (`show_calls_chosen`), otherwise on until onboarding completes | The prototype has no calls panel; DESIGN-V2 §3 asked for it; secrets must never reach a snapshot (I6) |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:68:| P55 | Test Cases with per-case results and pack names (prototype `results`, `packCases`) | A case carries its starter pack's name (set when the bundled catalog's cases are added, at onboarding completion or by Add starter packs) and its last result: the verdict of a Playground scan opened from the case, recorded by the core when the scan settles and only while the case still holds the prompt and response that were scanned (`TestCase.result`, `Intent::Scan.case`). Neither is ever exported or imported (v1's file format is unchanged); a copy to another tenant drops the result. A case can be named by a scan only if it is a Runtime Security case of the tenant's open library (otherwise the scan is refused before anything is sent); a result stored on any other case is dropped when the library is read. Absent result: Not run. The library's workflows are kept in the v2 screen: New case, Add starter packs, Add public examples, Import…, Export…, Remove and explicit Copy to another tenant, with the library's unsaved, failure, problem and notice states and their recovery (Save again, Open the library again). Search and the product filter run over name, pack and input; pages of 20 (at most 32 cases per tenant, v1's limit) | The library had no result or pack; the prototype's results were UI state |
docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md:69:| P56 | "Open in Playground" sets the Playground's prompt, direction and profile | It hands the case's profile, prompt, response and application to Administration's Runtime Playground (the v2 home of the Runtime scan form) and opens that tab; the operator runs the scan there, and that scan names the case. The scan form has no separate direction: a case's prompt and response are its directions. Nothing is scanned by opening it, and Inspect dispatches nothing | The Runtime scan form (C03) is the admitted execution surface; direction is expressed by which fields hold text |
